1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,710
In strong earthquakes, soil softens —

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:06,430
yet efficient methods model it

3
00:00:06,430 --> 00:00:08,840
as a one-dimensional column.

4
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:12,965
But real seismic waves are three-dimensional,

5
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:16,285
and fully nonlinear time-domain simulations

6
00:00:16,285 --> 00:00:17,550
are costly.

7
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:23,060
We extend the hybrid frequency–time method to 3D.

8
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:26,040
An integral transform solution

9
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:28,470
delivers three-dimensional strains,

10
00:00:28,470 --> 00:00:31,215
and soil stiffness and damping update

11
00:00:31,215 --> 00:00:33,130
until they converge.

12
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:36,890
It handles spatially varying input,

13
00:00:36,890 --> 00:00:39,090
and matches the 1D reference

14
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:40,760
for plane waves.

15
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:42,395
Next:

16
00:00:42,395 --> 00:00:44,190
anisotropic soils

17
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:46,770
and 3D material models.
